"[2602.02987v1] Large-Scale LLM Inference with Heterogeneous Workloads: Prefill-Decode Contention and Asymptotically Optimal Control (https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.02987v1)\nciteturn31530view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.02987v1\",\"lineno\":0}); Total lines: 163\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 3 Feb 2026 (this version), latest version 4 Jun 2026 (cite5†v2 )]\nL9: # Title:Large-Scale LLM Inference with Heterogeneous Workloads: Prefill-Decode Contention and Asymptotically Optimal Control\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Ruihan Lin , cite7†Zezhen Ding , cite8†Zean Han , cite9†Jiheng Zhang L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Large-Scale LLM Inference with Heterogeneous Workloads: Prefill-Decode Contention and Asymptotically Optimal Control, by Ruihan Lin and 3 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite10†View PDF cite11†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Large Language Models (LLMs) are rapidly becoming critical infrastructure for enterprise applications, driving unprecedented demand for GPU-based inference services. A key operational challenge arises from the two-phase nature of LLM inference: a compute-intensive \\emph{prefill} phase that processes user input, followed by a memory-bound \\emph{decode} phase that generates output tokens.\nL17: When these phases share GPU resources, prefill tasks throttle the processing speed of concurrent decodes, creating state-dependent contention. This contention is further complicated by workload heterogeneity, as different applications exhibit vastly different input and output lengths. We develop a stochastic control framework for scheduling heterogeneous LLM workloads across large GPU clusters.\nL18: We formulate LLM inference as a multiclass many-server queueing network with state-dependent service rates, grounded in empirical iteration-time measurements. We analyze the fluid approximation of this system and solve steady-state linear programs that characterize optimal resource allocation. We design gate-and-route policies that regulate prefill admission and decode routing, and prove that they are asymptotically optimal in the many-GPU limit under both bundled and separate token-pricing schemes.\nL19: We further extend the framework to incorporate Service Level Indicators (SLIs) such as latency and fairness, providing a general approach to constrained scheduling. Numerical experiments calibrated to empirical iteration-time data demonstrate that our policies outperform standard serving heuristics.\nL20: Subjects:  | Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC); Optimization and Control (math.OC)\nL21: Cite as:  | cite12†arXiv:2602.02987 [cs.DC]\nL22:    | (or cite13†arXiv:2602.02987v1 [cs.DC] for this version)\nL23:    | cite14†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2602.02987†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL24: ## Submission history\nL25: \nL26: From: Jiheng Zhang [cite15†view email ]\nL27: [v1] Tue, 3 Feb 2026 01:47:37 UTC (18,288 KB)\nL28: cite5†[v2] Thu, 4 Jun 2026 03:39:17 UTC (1,095 KB)\nL29: \nL30: Full-text links:\nL31: \nL32: ## Access Paper:\nL33: \nL34: View a PDF of the paper titled Large-Scale LLM Inference with Heterogeneous Workloads: Prefill-Decode Contention and Asymptotically Optimal Control, by Ruihan Lin and 3 other authors\nL35: \nL36:   * cite10†View PDF L37:   * cite11†HTML (experimental) L38:   * cite16†TeX Source L39: \nL40: cite17†view license L41: ### Current browse context:\nL42: \nL43: cs.DC\nL44: \nL45: cite18†< prev |   cite19†next > L46: \nL47: cite20†new | cite21†recent | cite22†2026-02 L48: \nL49: Change t